  Findings: This series of images documents the progressive resolution of a diffuse, erythematous dermatosis affecting the bilateral lower extremities. At baseline (D0), the lesions present as confluent, erythematous plaques with prominent scaling and superficial desquamation, particularly evident on the anterior and lateral aspects of the thighs and lower legs. The coloration is predominantly bright red to pink, with areas of hyperkeratotic scaling that appear silvery-white, suggesting a chronic inflammatory or psoriasiform process. The borders are irregular and poorly defined, with some areas fading into surrounding normal skin, indicating a non-circumscribed, inflammatory distribution. Surface texture is characterized by thick, adherent scaling and mild lichenification, with no evidence of ulceration, crusting, or telangiectasia. The global structure is symmetrically distributed bilaterally, with no discernible pigment network, globules, or regression patterns. Over the course of 30 days (D1 to D30), there is a marked improvement: erythema resolves significantly, scaling diminishes, and skin texture normalizes, suggesting a favorable response to therapeutic intervention. The progression from D0 to D30 demonstrates a clear temporal resolution of inflammation and epidermal hyperplasia, consistent with a self-limiting or treatable dermatitis.
